  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: VERY humid conditions continue early this afternoon, \nbut a wide array of temperatures in the region. Early morning \nstratus and fog has kept a lid on temperatures north of I-90, but \nsouth of the Interstate we've seen highs climb towards the 90s. Heat \nindex in a few areas may reach the 100 degree mark. A couple\npatches of ACCAS based AOA 600 mb continue to move along I-90 \nthis afternoon, but development potential remains low as this \nmoves into NW Iowa. Will continue to monitor. \n\nTHIS EVENING/TONIGHT: CAMS continue to struggle resolving the myriad \nof weak forcing waves moving through the Plains, and these trends \ncontinue today.  The result is sporadic convection developing over \nthe western High Plains in the late afternoon and evening and then \ngradual tracks southeast overnight.  Despite the high level of \ninstability across the Dakotas, very few strong focusing \nmechanisms for convection remain apparent this afternoon. What \nmay happen is gradual upslope flow over the western Dakotas \ncombined with increasing SE 850mb flow could induce a cluster or\ntwo of storms. This activity will tend to follow the mean flow \nand push east southeast into the overnight hours. However, a \ncloser look at soundings continues to show fairly strong \ninhibition both to surface based parcels but also to elevated \nparcels. So with that in mind, planning on keeping PoPs low, \nwith some focus along I-90 and areas moreso over the Missouri \nRiver valley. Continue to monitor trends this evening, as strong\nstorms could bring some higher wind gusts if they survive. \n\nHigher confidence again in the development of stratus and fog given \nthe continued light northeast wind and trapped boundary layer \nmoisture.  Much like this morning, areas of locally dense fog may \nform with widespread stratus potential possible.  \n\nWEDNESDAY-WENDESDAY NIGHT: Assuming stratus/fog does not hinder the \nwarmup tomorrow significantly, we'll continue to see another warm \nafternoon on Wednesday with dry conditions. Dew points will remain \nin the 70s, which should cap high temperatures in to the middle\n80s. A slight shift in the mid-lvl flow begins Wednesday, with \npersistent ridging over the Four Corners translating eastward \ninto the Southern Plains through the day. This will result in \npulling a sub-tropical moisture plume with embedded vorticity \nnorthward through the Rockies and into the Plains late in the \nday and overnight. Slightly better signals for convection tied \nto this wave will begin to focus over western SD/NE late in the \nafternoon, with rain gradually shifting eastward into Thursday \nmorning. The severe weather risks remain on the lower side but \nnot non-zero as some lingering wind threat could present itself \nalong the MO river valley. Mid-lvl lapse rates quickly worsen as\nyou move into the overnight hours however. One noticeable thing\nto watch is the increasing PWAT values which approach 1.75\" by \nThursday morning. This could signal at least the potential for \nhighly localized heavier rain amounts, which the HREF PMM \nsignals in the 2-4\" range. The key to watch will be where the \nresidual surface boundary falls by late Wednesday, as this could\npull better convection risks south of the CWA. \n\nTHURSDAY-FRIDAY: Quite a bit of cloud cover may be in the area for \nThursday as cooler northeasterly flow returns and we'll be on the \nedge of the baroclinic zone to our south.  At this point, highs in \nthe 70s are likely, with a gradually increasing rain chance into \nFriday as mid-lvl flow shifts back to the southwest, mid-lvl \nvorticity slides through the Central Plains, and the baroclinic zone \nlifts northward. The highest PoPs will be focused Thursday night \ninto Friday.\n\nSATURDAY-MONDAY: A flattened mid-lvl ridge remains over the central \nCONUS into the upcoming weekend, bringing another risk for rain by \nSaturday as energy tracks back northward into the Upper Midwest. \nConfidence is rising that we'll see a dry Sunday and Monday as high \npressure arrives, mid-lvl ridging shifts back westward, and quasi-\nnorthwest flow moves back in.  Temperatures may begin to rise back \ninto the 80s by Sunday and Monday.",
